      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Painting a room"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "To buy paint you need the area of the walls; to fill a tank you need its volume. Heron's formula gives the area of a triangle from its three sides alone, and the cuboid and cube formulae give surface areas and volumes."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "Heron's formula",
          "13, 14, 15",
          "cuboid",
          "cube",
          "diagonal",
          "units"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Heron's formula: area of a triangle with sides a, b, c is the square root of [s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)] where s = (a + b + c)/2.\nFor a triangle with sides 13, 14, 15: s = 21, area = root(21 x 8 x 7 x 6) = 84 square units.\nCuboid of length l, breadth b, height h: total surface area = 2(lb + bh + hl); lateral surface area = 2h(l + b); volume = l x b x h.\nCube of side a: total surface area = 6 a squared, lateral = 4 a squared, volume = a cubed.\nThe diagonal of a cuboid is root(l^2 + b^2 + h^2).\nVolume is in cubic units, area in square units; litres: 1000 cubic cm = 1 litre."
        }
      },
